Compare answers with labelled examples. Calibrate never chooses a threshold.

Usage:
  onesie calibrate [question] [flags]

Asking

Flags:
      --ask NAME=QUESTION       Ask a named question. Repeat for several.
      --pick OPTIONS            Choose from comma separated options.
      --rate LEVELS             Rate against levels from lowest to highest.
  -f, --file FILE               Load saved questions, a request body or a built in
                                starter set.
      --for ID                  ID, repeatable, selects a question from -f, so the
                                --pick, --rate, --desc and --sep after it apply to that
                                question
      --desc KEY=TEXT           Describe an option, level, yes or no.
      --sep STRING              separator for --pick and --rate that follow it
      --box                     make this a box question, answered with the left, top,
                                right and bottom edges of what it finds in an image,
                                each from 0 to 1. Takes no value. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side
      --replace                 --ask overrides an id from -f

How they work together:
  A positional question has the id answer. By default a question asks for a yes or no
  probability. Use --pick for a choice, --rate for ordered levels, or --box for image
  edges. A rate score is the level index averaged over p, from 0, and norm is score over
  the top index, from 0 to 1. Box edges are left, top, right and bottom, each from 0 to
  1. Box is experimental on TypeSafe's side. See Input for image restrictions.

  Question flags apply to the preceding --ask or --for. Flags before any group apply to
  the one question asked, whether positional, a lone --ask or a file with one question.
  Beside several questions they need --for ID. --ask NAME=QUESTION opens a named group
  and may repeat. --for ID selects a question from --file. Root runs issue a notice for
  each setting overriding a selected file question. Repeated --pick adds options and
  repeated --rate adds levels after earlier ones. Together they replace the file
  question's options or levels. --desc KEY=TEXT replaces only that option, level, yes or
  no description. A rate needs every level described or none. --sep changes the
  separator for following --pick and --rate. --for may repeat and keeps the wording and
  settings that no flag changes. It needs a file and an existing id and refuses
  --replace. Flags cannot change a file question shape.

  --file accepts a question file, a request body, a path or a saved name. Saved names
  are looked up in .onesie/questions, then the config directory, then built in starter
  sets. --ask needs --replace to replace an id from the file. This replaces the
  question, while --for changes selected settings.

  A question that begins with a dash needs -- before it, with all flags first, as in
  onesie -o json -- '-is this urgent'. Use the same escape for a one word question close
  to a command name, since onesie questoins exits 2 and suggests questions. On
  calibration, --for accepts only --pick, --rate, --desc and --sep. Box questions cannot
  be calibrated.

Input

Flags:
  -i, --input FORMAT            required, jsonl, csv or tsv
      --map EXPR                jq expression run on each record, whose result is the
                                state sent. Map only the text a person would read
      --id EXPR                 jq expression run on each record, whose string or number
                                result names it
      --delimiter STRING        field separator for -i csv: auto, or one character.
                                Defaults to a comma
      --image NAME=PATH         NAME=PATH, repeatable, puts a PNG or JPEG in every
                                record's state under NAME. Experimental on TypeSafe's
                                side
      --image-field NAME        repeatable, a state key whose value is a path to a PNG
                                or JPEG, read per record. Experimental on TypeSafe's
                                side
      --skip-blank              drop blank lines

How they work together:
  Calibration requires JSONL, CSV or TSV records on stdin. --map runs a jq expression on
  each original record and sends its result as the state. Map only the text a person
  would read. Object keys come out sorted. The result may nest max-map-depth levels deep
  and encode to max-line-bytes, as version lists. Labels are selected from the original
  record.

  --id runs one record at a time as input is read. Its result must be a string or
  number. Keep it cheap, such as a field lookup. version lists max-id-bytes.
  --skip-blank drops blank lines without emitting a result. The default field separator
  is ','. --delimiter auto detects the separator of CSV input. An explicit delimiter
  takes one character. TSV uses tabs.

  --image NAME=PATH adds a PNG or JPEG under NAME. Streams read stdin and add the fixed
  image to every record. --image-field reads a PNG or JPEG path from a state key per
  record. Image support is experimental on the TypeSafe side. See version for image,
  file, token and model limits.

Evaluation

Flags:
      --label [ID=]EXPR         [ID=]EXPR, a jq expression run on the record, not on the
                                mapped state, whose result is the right answer for
                                question ID. A question with none is skipped. With one
                                question ID= may be left out
      --cuts STRING             comma separated cuts between 0 and 1. See Evaluation for
                                defaults
      --require CONDITION       [lower|upper](ID.MEASURE) OP NUMBER [at CUT], a
                                requirement the report must meet, or calibrate exits 1.
                                Repeatable
      --offline                 with --out and --resume, read every answer from the file
                                and ask nothing, needing no key

How they work together:
  Calibration compares answers with labels from the original record, not the mapped
  state, and prints cut, agreement and confusion tables. It never picks the cut. A --map
  selecting the label flatters the question. --label can omit ID= with one question.
  Default cuts: 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, 0.25, 0.3, 0.35, 0.4, 0.45, 0.5, 0.55, 0.6, 0.65,
  0.7, 0.75, 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95.

  A yes or no label is true, false, yes, no, y, n, 1 or 0 in any case. Pick and rate
  labels are declared option or level names, with surrounding spaces ignored, or a
  number whose text names one, so 4 matches --rate 1,2,3,4,5. null, no result or a
  string of only spaces leaves that record unlabelled for the question. A record no
  question labels is not asked. A JSONL record can be:

    {"id":"a","text":"The server is down","urgent":true}

  CSV can use:

    id,text,urgent
    a,The server is down,yes

  A question with no --label is skipped, omitted from requests and the report, and named
  on stderr. At least one question needs a label. A label naming no question or a
  requirement reading a skipped question exits 2. Resume into a file answering every
  question still asks skipped ones so the file stays whole. Box questions cannot be
  calibrated. A question file's assert, abstain_if, threshold, min_confidence and
  fallback are ignored. The corresponding root flags are refused.

  A yes or no cut flags a record when value is at least the cut. Pick and rate tables
  have a row per name. found is the share labelled with a name that were picked as it.
  right when picked is the share picked as a name carrying it as their label.

  Each --require reads [lower|upper](ID.MEASURE) OP NUMBER [at CUT]. MEASURE is catches,
  false_alarms, right_when_flagged or auc for yes or no, agreement for pick and rate,
  and within_one for rate. OP is >=, >, <= or <. NUMBER is a fraction from 0 to 1. lower
  and upper compare an end of the 95 percent interval instead of the value, so 16 of 16
  fails lower(x.catches) >= 0.9. A yes or no measure other than auc needs a cut. at CUT
  names it, at abstain takes it from the file's abstain_if, otherwise the file's assert
  supplies it when comparing ID.value < X or ID.value >= X. A missing cut exits 2. The
  report still prints when requirements fail, each failure is named on stderr and the
  run exits 1.

  --offline needs --out and --resume, reads every answer from the file and asks nothing.
  It needs no key and never rewrites the file. A missing answer, missing file or
  fingerprint mismatch exits 2. The first example creates the answer file used by the
  second. The example requirement is illustrative. Offline replay checks saved evidence,
  not new live model behavior.

  Compare answers with labelled examples:
    onesie calibrate --ask 'urgent=is this urgent' -i jsonl --map .text --label urgent=.urgent --id .id --out answers.jsonl --resume < labelled.jsonl

  Check the saved answers without asking again:
    onesie calibrate --ask 'urgent=is this urgent' -i jsonl --map .text --label urgent=.urgent --id .id --out answers.jsonl --resume --offline --require 'urgent.catches >= 0.9 at 0.5' < labelled.jsonl

Output

Flags:
      --out STRING              write the answers to this file as -o json lines
  -o, --output FORMAT           the report, table, json or auto, which means table
      --usage                   add the api usage object to the answers file and the
                                json report
      --stats                   write a one line summary of the run to stderr at exit
  -h, --help                    help for calibrate

How they work together:
  --output selects the report format, table, json or auto. Auto means table. --out
  selects a separate answer file, written as JSON lines, and does not redirect the
  report. --usage adds API usage to both the answer file and JSON report. --stats writes
  a one line run summary to stderr at exit. See Execution for answer file resume and
  pruning.

Connections

Flags:
      --provider STRING         run on this provider without a profile, typesafe,
                                openrouter, berget, levanto or cloudflare. Overrides
                                ONESIE_PROVIDER and the default profile. Refused beside
                                --profile or ONESIE_PROFILE
      --profile STRING          the profile a run uses, from profiles.json in the config
                                dir. Overrides ONESIE_PROFILE and ONESIE_PROVIDER, and
                                otherwise the default profile runs, which onesie profile
                                use sets
  -m, --model STRING            model override
      --base-url STRING         api root override. Also ONESIE_BASE_URL
      --allow-http              send the request, and any API key, over a plain http
                                base URL to a host other than this machine. Without it
                                such a base URL exits 2 before any request

How they work together:
  onesie is a Unix filter over the System One API, served by typesafe, openrouter,
  berget, levanto or cloudflare. A profile in profiles.json holds a provider, a key in
  the OS keychain or file, and optionally a model and base URL or cloudflare account.
  onesie profile create stores one and onesie profile use makes it the default.

  A run uses the profile named by --profile, then ONESIE_PROFILE, then the default
  profile. --profile overrides ONESIE_PROVIDER. --provider runs without a profile and
  overrides ONESIE_PROVIDER and the default profile. It is refused beside --profile or
  ONESIE_PROFILE. ONESIE_PROVIDER beside ONESIE_PROFILE is also refused. With no
  profiles, typesafe runs directly. Provider keys come from TYPESAFE_API_KEY,
  OPENROUTER_API_KEY, BERGET_API_KEY, LEVANTO_API_KEY or CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. The
  chosen profile key and endpoint beat the environment. Provider variables beat the
  default profile. --base-url wins over a chosen profile, then ONESIE_BASE_URL, then the
  provider base URL or account variable, then the default profile. On typesafe,
  ONESIE_BASE_URL beats TYPESAFE_BASE_URL.

  A key goes only with its own endpoint. A custom profile endpoint takes only the key
  from that profile. A custom URL from a variable takes only the provider key variable.
  A typed --base-url takes the chosen profile key or provider variable, never the
  default profile key. A keyless profile reads no key variable. No provider uses another
  provider key. The base URL and account are one setting, taken whole from the highest
  source.

  On cloudflare an account comes from the profile, CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID or a base URL
  holding it. --model overrides the chosen profile or request body.
  TYPESAFE_DEFAULT_MODEL applies when none names a model and beats the model in the
  default profile. A custom base URL needs no key and sends none when none resolves.
  Plain HTTP to a host other than this machine needs --allow-http, which permits sending
  the request and any API key over it. Without that flag the run exits 2 before a
  request. A profile storing such a URL needs --allow-http on every run too.

Execution

Flags:
      --resume                  with --out and --id, skip the ids the file already
                                answers
      --print-request           write api shaped request bodies to stdout and exit
      --prune                   with --resume, drop the answered ids the input no longer
                                has
      --long-context            raise the context limits to 262k tokens, slower and less
                                accurate. Also ONESIE_LONG_CONTEXT=1. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side
      --mock FILE               answer from this file instead of the API. Also
                                ONESIE_MOCK. The file is an object keyed by question id,
                                as {"answer":0.9,"team":"billing"}, or one line per
                                record in the shape -o json writes. A lone line that
                                carries id, line, model, usage, assert, abstain or an
                                error object is one record
      --cache                   store responses on disk and answer a repeated request
                                from them. Also ONESIE_CACHE=1
  -j, --jobs INT                records in flight at once. Default: 1.
      --timeout INT             seconds per attempt, 60 for a request with images unless
                                given. Default: 10.
      --retries INT             retries after a failed attempt. Default: 2.
      --max-retry-after INT     most seconds to wait out a server Retry-After. Default:
                                60.

How they work together:
  --jobs bounds records in flight. --timeout is seconds per attempt. Requests with
  images default to 60 seconds unless given. Retries happen after failed attempts,
  bounded by --retries. --max-retry-after caps seconds spent waiting for a server
  Retry-After. --long-context raises context limits, is slower and less accurate, and is
  experimental on TypeSafe's side. version reports the built in limits.

  --cache, or ONESIE_CACHE=1, stores successful responses on disk and reuses repeated
  requests. onesie cache reports entries and onesie cache clear empties them.

  Calibration resume needs --out and --id, and skips answered ids. A record whose
  content changes but whose id does not keeps its old answer. The file is rewritten in
  input order. Answered ids absent from input stay after them unless --prune drops them.

  --mock FILE, or ONESIE_MOCK=FILE, answers from fixtures without a key or network, so
  gate scripts can be tested for free. --print-request writes API shaped request bodies
  and sends none.

  Changing a label or --cuts reuses stored answers. A plain stream can resume the file
  with the same questions, model, input format, --map and --id, with -o json and no gate
  or merge. Offline reads the saved answers without rewriting them. It validates
  recorded evidence and does not detect new live model behavior.

Exit codes:
  0    every record was answered and every --require held
  1    a --require did not hold
  2    usage or validation error, including a bad label or record
  3    the key was refused, the account is out of credits, the keychain failed, or
       profiles.json or credentials.json is exposed. No report prints
  6    the report holds one or more failed records, which wins over 1
  130  interrupted. No report prints
